<div class="wrap">
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Embed</span>
    <h1>Scores on any website</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      eui-score also comes as <strong>WebAssembly</strong>: the same engraver, compiled for the browser. One
      <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag, one <code>&lt;eui-score&gt;</code> tag, and your page engraves LilyPond by
      itself — no server, no account, nothing to install. Every score on this page is engraved that way, in your
      browser.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="doc-layout">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="toc-title">On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#start">In two tags</a></li>
        <li><a href="#why-script">Why a &lt;script&gt;</a></li>
        <li><a href="#file">From a .ly file</a></li>
        <li><a href="#size">Size and width</a></li>
        <li><a href="#colour">Colour</a></li>
        <li><a href="#api">The JavaScript API</a></li>
        <li><a href="#events">Events</a></li>
        <li><a href="#editor">An editable score</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frameworks">React, Vue, Svelte…</a></li>
        <li><a href="#self-host">Hosting it yourself</a></li>
        <li><a href="#server">Or on the server</a></li>
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <div class="prose">
      <section class="doc-section" id="start">
        <h2>In two tags<a class="anchor" href="#start">#</a></h2>
        <p>Load the module once, anywhere in the page:</p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;script type="module" src="https://score.solisoft.net/eui-score.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Then put a score where it should appear:</p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;eui-score&gt;
  &lt;script type="text/x-lilypond"&gt;
    \header { title = "Minuet" composer = "J. S. Bach" }
    \relative c'' { \key g \major \time 3/4
      d4( g,8 a b c | d4) g,-. g-. | e'4( c8 d e fis | g4) g,-. g-. \bar "|." }
  &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/eui-score&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Which gives, engraved just now by your browser:</p>
        <div class="score-sheet">
          <eui-score>
            <script type="text/x-lilypond">
              \header { title = "Minuet" composer = "J. S. Bach" }
              \relative c'' { \key g \major \time 3/4
                d4( g,8 a b c | d4) g,-. g-. | e'4( c8 d e fis | g4) g,-. g-. \bar "|." }
            </script>
          </eui-score>
        </div>
        <p>
          The module is 26 KB of JavaScript; the engraver, <strong>826 KB of WebAssembly (290 KB gzipped)</strong>,
          is downloaded once and cached, however many scores the page holds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="why-script">
        <h2>Why a &lt;script type="text/x-lilypond"&gt;<a class="anchor" href="#why-script">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          LilyPond is full of angle brackets: <code>&lt;c e g&gt;</code> is a chord, <code>&lt;&lt; … &gt;&gt;</code>
          joins staves. Written as they are in a page, the browser would read them as HTML tags and the source would
          arrive mangled. The content of a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> of an unknown type is neither run nor parsed:
          it arrives intact. A source without angle brackets may also be the element's own text.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;eui-score&gt;\relative c' { c4 d e f | g1 \bar "|." }&lt;/eui-score&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="score-sheet"><eui-score>\relative c' { c4 d e f | g1 \bar "|." }</eui-score></div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="file">
        <h2>From a .ly file<a class="anchor" href="#file">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>src</code> fetches the source: a file on your site, or any address that allows cross-origin
          reads. The gallery's pieces are served that way, at
          <code>/examples/&lt;family&gt;/&lt;piece&gt;/source.ly</code>:
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;eui-score src="https://score.solisoft.net/examples/ensembles/string-quartet/source.ly"&gt;&lt;/eui-score&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="score-sheet"><eui-score src="<%= example_source_path("ensembles", "string-quartet") %>"></eui-score></div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="size">
        <h2>Size and width<a class="anchor" href="#size">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          By default the score takes the width of its element, and is engraved again when that changes: bars are
          broken into systems that fit. <code>staff-space</code> sets the size (px between two staff lines, 7 by
          default); <code>width</code> fixes a width in px.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;eui-score staff-space="10" src="…"&gt;&lt;/eui-score&gt;
&lt;eui-score width="420" src="…"&gt;&lt;/eui-score&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>
          <label for="embed-size">staff-space: <strong id="embed-size-value">7</strong> px</label><br>
          <input id="embed-size" type="range" min="4" max="14" step="0.5" value="7" style="width: 100%; max-width: 360px">
        </p>
        <div class="score-sheet"><eui-score id="embed-sized" src="<%= example_source_path("piano", "minuet-in-g") %>"></eui-score></div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="colour">
        <h2>Colour<a class="anchor" href="#colour">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          The ink is <code>currentColor</code>: the score takes the colour of the text around it, so it follows a
          light or dark theme with nothing to do.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;eui-score style="color: #8b2b2c"&gt;…&lt;/eui-score&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="score-sheet" style="color: #8b2b2c">
          <eui-score><script type="text/x-lilypond">\relative c'' { \key d \major fis4\mf fis g a | a g fis e | d d e fis | e4. d8 d2 \bar "|." }</script></eui-score>
        </div>
        <div class="score-sheet" style="background: #1d1914; color: #f3efe6; margin-top: 12px">
          <eui-score><script type="text/x-lilypond">\relative c'' { \key d \major fis4\mf fis g a | a g fis e | d d e fis | e4. d8 d2 \bar "|." }</script></eui-score>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="api">
        <h2>The JavaScript API<a class="anchor" href="#api">#</a></h2>
        <p>To drive the engraving from your own code, the module exports its functions:</p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>import { render, toMidi, toPdf, check, download }
  from "https://score.solisoft.net/eui-score.js";

const svg = await render(source, { width: 640, staffSpace: 7, marks: { "2": "crimson" } });
element.innerHTML = svg;                          // an SVG string

const midi = await toMidi(source, { bpm: 96 });   // a Uint8Array, a Standard MIDI File
download(midi, "minuet.mid", "audio/midi");

const pdf = await toPdf(source, "Minuet");        // the A4 pages, as a PDF
download(pdf, "minuet.pdf", "application/pdf");

await check("{ c'64 }");   // "score: the duration 64 is not one this draws (…) (at character 5)"</code></pre>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>render(src, opts)</code></td><td>The SVG. <code>width</code>, <code>staffSpace</code>, and <code>marks</code>: item number (notes and rests, from 0) → a CSS colour. Rejects with the engraver's refusal.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>toMidi(src, opts)</code></td><td>The MIDI file, played as the editor plays it: every repeat played out, its endings in turn, D.S. and D.C. taken (for a source the editor can open; another plays as written). <code>bpm</code> and <code>beat</code> for a source with no tempo (100 a quarter by default), <code>program</code> for a staff with no instrument, <code>unfold: false</code> to play it as written.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>toPdf(src, title)</code></td><td>The vector A4 PDF, as a string.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>check(src)</code></td><td>The refusal, or <code>""</code> when the source can be engraved.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>download(data, name, type)</code></td><td>Offers a file to save.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>loaded()</code></td><td>A promise that resolves once the WebAssembly is ready.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Try it: <button type="button" class="score-btn" id="embed-pdf">the minuet's PDF, made here</button>
          <button type="button" class="score-btn" id="embed-midi">its MIDI</button>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="events">
        <h2>Events<a class="anchor" href="#events">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          The element fires <code>render</code> after each engraving (<code>detail.width</code>, and
          <code>detail.ms</code> it took) and <code>refuse</code> when the source is refused (<code>detail</code>:
          the message, which is also shown in place of the score). Its <code>source</code> property reads and
          replaces the LilyPond: that is all a live editor needs, like the <a href="<%= playground_path() %>">playground</a>.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>const score = document.querySelector("eui-score");
textarea.addEventListener("input", () =&gt; { score.source = textarea.value; });
score.addEventListener("refuse", (event) =&gt; console.warn(event.detail));</code></pre>
        <div class="score-sheet score-sheet-refused">
          <eui-score><script type="text/x-lilypond">{ c'4 d'4 \glissando e'4 }</script></eui-score>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="editor">
        <h2>An editable score<a class="anchor" href="#editor">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;eui-score-editor&gt;</code> is the same score, editable with the mouse and the keyboard. Click a
          note — this one is live:
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;script type="module" src="https://score.solisoft.net/eui-score-editor.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;eui-score-editor&gt;
  &lt;script type="text/x-lilypond"&gt;{ \key g \major \time 3/4 g'4 a' b' | d''2 r4 | &lt;g' b'&gt;2. \bar "|." }&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/eui-score-editor&gt;</code></pre>
        <div class="score-sheet">
          <eui-score-editor id="embed-editor"><script type="text/x-lilypond">{ \key g \major \time 3/4 g'4 a' b' | d''2 r4 | <g' b'>2. \bar "|." }</script></eui-score-editor>
        </div>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Do</th><th>To</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Click, <code>←</code> <code>→</code></td><td>Select a note or a rest. <code>Alt+↑</code> <code>Alt+↓</code> choose one note of a chord.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code>, or drag</td><td>Move the note a step in the key; with <code>Shift</code> a semitone, with <code>Ctrl</code> an octave.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>A</code>–<code>G</code></td><td>Write that note in place of the selection: a rest becomes a note of its length.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Shift</code>+<code>A</code>–<code>G</code></td><td>Add that note above the chord.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>R</code>, <code>Delete</code></td><td>The note becomes a rest; a chord's selected note leaves the chord.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>+</code> <code>−</code> <code>=</code></td><td>Sharp, flat, natural.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Ctrl+Z</code>, <code>Ctrl+Y</code></td><td>Undo, redo. <strong>+ Bar</strong> adds a bar of rests at the end, to write into.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The LilyPond text stays the only state. Each edit is made by eui-score's own edit functions — those EUI's
          score editor uses — which rewrite only the characters that spell a note, and each result is engraved
          before it is kept: an edit the engraver would refuse is refused, with its reason. Notes are written in place
          of rests, as in MuseScore, so a bar never runs over. A source in <code>\relative</code> is written out in
          absolute pitches on its first edit, since a relative pitch depends on the notes before it.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>const editor = document.querySelector("eui-score-editor");
editor.addEventListener("change", (event) =&gt; save(event.detail.source));
editor.source = "{ c'4 d' e' f' }";       // replace the music
editor.edit({ op: "move", item: 0, how: "step", by: 1 });   // or edit it from code</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="frameworks">
        <h2>React, Vue, Svelte…<a class="anchor" href="#frameworks">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;eui-score&gt;</code> is a standard HTML element (a <em>custom element</em>): any framework that
          can write HTML can use it. Pass the source through the <code>source</code> property, or call
          <code>render()</code> and place the SVG yourself.
        </p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>// React
function Score({ source }) {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() =&gt; { ref.current.source = source; }, [source]);
  return &lt;eui-score ref={ref} /&gt;;
}</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="self-host">
        <h2>Hosting it yourself<a class="anchor" href="#self-host">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          The files, side by side in any static folder; the module finds the others next to itself, under the version
          it was loaded with (<code>?v=…</code>), so a cache never mixes two versions:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/eui-score/eui-score.js?v=<%= eui_version %>" download>eui-score.js</a> — the element and the API;</li>
          <li><a href="/eui-score/eui-score-editor.js?v=<%= eui_version %>" download>eui-score-editor.js</a> — the editable score, optional;</li>
          <li><a href="/eui-score/score-model.js?v=<%= eui_version %>" download>score-model.js</a> — plays repeats out in the MIDI, fetched only for a piece that has some; optional;</li>
          <li><a href="/eui-score/eui_score_web.js?v=<%= eui_version %>" download>eui_score_web.js</a> — the binding generated by wasm-bindgen;</li>
          <li><a href="/eui-score/eui_score_web_bg.wasm?v=<%= eui_version %>" download>eui_score_web_bg.wasm</a> — the engraver.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Serve the <code>.wasm</code> as <code>application/wasm</code> (almost every server does). From another
          origin than yours, the files need an <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> header: score.solisoft.net
          sends one for them and for the gallery's sources. Its addresses <code>/eui-score.js</code> and
          <code>/eui-score-editor.js</code> always load the current version, and are cached five minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The module is built from the <code>eui-score</code> crate by <code>wasm/build.sh</code>
          (<code>wasm-bindgen</code>, target <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>): the same code EUI's
          <code>score()</code> widget and Soli's native functions call.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="server">
        <h2>Or on the server<a class="anchor" href="#server">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          Engraving in the browser means downloading the engraver. For a page that must show without JavaScript or
          be indexed — like this site's <a href="<%= examples_path() %>">gallery</a> — a Soli application engraves
          on the server with <code>eui_score_layout</code> and <code>eui_score_bands</code> (see the
          <a href="<%= guide_path() %>#server">guide</a>). The drawing is the same to the pixel: it is the same
          engraver.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The version beside the script, not in it: its text never changes (#544). %>
<%- page_data("embed-data", {"v": eui_version}) %>
<script type="module">
  const { v } = JSON.parse(document.getElementById("embed-data").textContent);
  const { toPdf, toMidi, download } = await import(`/eui-score/eui-score.js?v=${v}`);
  await import(`/eui-score/eui-score-editor.js?v=${v}`);

  const slider = document.getElementById("embed-size");
  const sized = document.getElementById("embed-sized");
  slider.addEventListener("input", () => {
    document.getElementById("embed-size-value").textContent = slider.value;
    sized.setAttribute("staff-space", slider.value);
  });

  const minuet = () => document.getElementById("embed-sized").source;
  document.getElementById("embed-pdf").addEventListener("click", async () => {
    download(await toPdf(minuet(), "Minuet"), "minuet.pdf", "application/pdf");
  });
  document.getElementById("embed-midi").addEventListener("click", async () => {
    download(await toMidi(minuet()), "minuet.mid", "audio/midi");
  });
</script>
